[{:db/id 17592186339811 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\,i-\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Interpret circulation as the sum of four convergent improper line integrals over the open quadrant arcs, with independent one-sided limits at $(\\pm1,0)$ and $(0,\\pm1)$. The field is undefined at those four points, so the original ordinary circulation is not defined." :question/worked-solution "The original field is undefined at the four axis intersections, so an ordinary line integral over the whole circle is not defined. For the explicit improper interpretation, $r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ traverses the unit circle counterclockwise. On each open quadrant arc,\n\n$$\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx=\\frac{-\\sin t}{\\sin t}\\,dt=-dt,\\qquad-\\frac{1}{x}\\,dy=\\frac{-\\cos t}{\\cos t}\\,dt=-dt.$$\n\nThus the scalar pullback is $-2$. Each endpoint has the finite one-sided limit $-2$, so each open-arc integral converges absolutely and equals $-\\pi$. Their sum is $\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{imp}}=-4\\pi$, the unchanged option a. No principal-value cancellation is used, and $F$ remains undefined at the excluded points." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
